# Git worktrees

Use an isolated worktree for every ticket branch. Do not do ticket work in the primary checkout on `main`.

## Layout

Worktrees live under the workspace (gitignored):

```
learn-english-journaling/            # primary checkout (stay on main)
  worktrees/
    <worktree-slug>/                 # one folder per branch
```

**`<worktree-slug>`** — branch name with `/` replaced by `-` (e.g. `feature/7-journal-topbar-header` → `feature-7-journal-topbar-header`).

`worktrees/` is listed in `.gitignore` — never commit its contents.

## Starting a branch

After choosing the branch name (see branch-naming rule), create the branch **and** worktree from `main` in the primary repo:

```bash
git fetch origin
mkdir -p worktrees
git worktree add -b <prefix>/<id>-<short-slug> \
  worktrees/<worktree-slug> \
  main
```

If the branch already exists remotely or locally:

```bash
mkdir -p worktrees
git worktree add \
  worktrees/<worktree-slug> \
  <prefix>/<id>-<short-slug>
```

Then open / continue work **inside that worktree path**.

## After a PR is merged

When the user says the PR merged (or merge is confirmed), clean up from the primary repo:

```bash
git worktree remove worktrees/<worktree-slug>
git worktree prune
git branch -d <prefix>/<id>-<short-slug>   # if still present locally
```

If the folder is gone or the worktree is broken:

```bash
git worktree prune
rm -rf worktrees/<worktree-slug>
```

## Rules

- One worktree per ticket branch; slug must match the branch.
- Keep the primary checkout on `main` (or the default base); use worktrees for feature/bug work.
- Create the worktree **before** making changes.
- Delete the worktree when the PR is merged — do not leave stale folders under `worktrees/`.
- Do not remove a worktree that still has uncommitted work without asking.

## Avoid

- Checking out feature branches in the primary repo when a worktree should be used
- Sibling `-wt` folders outside the workspace
- Reusing a worktree folder for a different branch
- Committing anything under `worktrees/`
